# BUILD OUTPUT IS NEVER TRACKED, AND THIS FILE IS WRITTEN TO MAKE THAT TRUE
# RATHER THAN INTENDED.
#
# Basalt ignores `build/` and `build-*/` and has eight build directories in its
# history named `bl-clang-address`, `v-gcc-16-thread` and so on -- committed
# binaries included, because the directories a human types under time pressure
# are never the ones the ignore file anticipated. The lesson taken here is not
# "add those eight names": it is that an ignore list which enumerates blessed
# prefixes fails open. So this file ignores the ARTIFACTS wherever they land, in
# addition to the directory names, and .github/workflows/ci.yml carries a job
# that fails if a tracked path looks like build output. The ignore file is the
# convenience; the CI job is the guarantee.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ by directory
# The names this project uses. `b-` is the prefix every documented command in
# this repo passes to `cmake -B`.
build/
build-*/
b-*/
out/
cmake-build-*/

# ------------------------------------------------------ desktop app, by name
# The same argument as above, for two toolchains that were not here before.
# Cargo puts 773 MB in `target/` on a first build and npm puts a node_modules
# beside it; neither is source and both are regenerable from a lockfile.
#
# `target/` is unanchored on purpose: cargo makes one wherever it is invoked,
# and a rule pinned to app/src-tauri/target would fail open the first time a
# second crate appears.
target/
node_modules/
*.app/
*.dmg
# Tauri regenerates src-tauri/gen from tauri.conf.json on every build, and the
# sidecar binaries under app/src-tauri/binaries are copies of cmake output.
# Both are build products that happen to live inside a source tree.
# ANCHORED PATTERNS FAIL OPEN, which is the warning at the top of this file
# meeting a fresh example: `gen/schemas/` contains a slash, so git anchors it to
# the repository root and it never matches app/src-tauri/gen/schemas. `**/` is
# what makes it mean "wherever this appears".
**/gen/schemas/
binaries/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- by artifact
# Named wherever they appear, because the directory list above is a courtesy and
# these are the things that must never be committed.
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
CTestTestfile.cmake
cmake_install.cmake
compile_commands.json
Makefile
Testing/
*.a
*.o
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.dSYM/
*.gcda
*.gcno
*.profraw
*.profdata

# `Makefile` above is a glob over the whole tree and there is no hand-written
# Makefile in this project. If one is ever added, negate it HERE and say so,
# rather than deleting the line: the line exists because CMake's Makefile
# generator writes one into every build directory, including directories whose
# names nobody predicted.

# --------------------------------------------------------------- vendored
#
# NOTHING UNDER third_party/ IS EVER IGNORED BY OUR RULES, and this is not
# belt-and-braces. `build-*/` above matched libsodium's `build-aux/` directory,
# which is upstream source, and twenty-four files went missing from the first
# vendoring attempt without a word. The tree-hash check in each VERSION file is
# what caught it.
#
# Two negations, because git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is
# excluded: the first un-excludes the directories, the second the files.
!/third_party/**/
!/third_party/**

# third_party/googletest is a vendored tree and IS tracked, whole. Its own
# .gitignore is upstream's file and is part of what we vendored; it is not
# consulted for paths under it because git only reads .gitignore files it finds
# while walking, and it will find that one. The exclusions it carries are for
# googletest's own build layout, which we never produce inside that tree.

# Logs. The lane scripts in this project write build and run logs beside the
# build directory they belong to, and a log is build output like any other.
*.log

# --------------------------------------------------------------- editors / OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- test
# The watcher suite builds its trees under a per-run temp directory obtained
# from TMPDIR and removes them; this catches the case where a test aborts under
# a sanitizer before its cleanup runs AND was pointed at the source tree.
/umbra-watch-test-*/
